              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em higher algebra
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
            refers to generalizations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and more general of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %h3#general General
          %p
            Ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            concerns itself in particular with structures such as
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                is just an ordinary
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
                is an ordinary algebra: a vector space equipped with a linear binary associative product with unit;
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
                is a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
          %p etc.
          %p Of course, there are other aspects to algebra such as those resulting from non-associative theories such as Lie algebras and there are many aspects such as questions within Galois theory, and representation theory for which the above is too limited a view, but for the moment let it stand.
          %p
            %strong Higher algebra
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-category"} higher categories
          %p A central motivating example for - or special case of the study of higher algebra was
          %ul
            %li
              monoids internal to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category+of+spectra"} stable (infinity,1)-category of spectra
              – called
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectrum"} commutative ring spectra
          %p
            The “higher algebra” embodied by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectrum"} commutative ring spectra
            has been called
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brave+new+algebra"} brave new algebra
            by F. Waldhausen.
          %p
            More generally,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            is partially about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            and about
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operads"} operads
          %h3#other_aspects_of_higher_algebra Other aspects of higher algebra?
          %p
            The parts of algebra that we set aside at the end of the
            %em idea
            are not outside the possible range of higher algebra, they just have not yet been that developed and it is not always clear in what directions they most naturally ‘should’ be developed. To take an example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+infinity-algebroid"} Lie infinity-algebroid
            is clearly a higher algebraic analogue of a Lie algebra, and is a ‘multi-object’ one as well. Questions in representation theory are often phrased in terms of monoidal categories, and their higher algebraic analogues have new structural facets that look very interesting and useful. Finally Galois theory naturally falls into the context of Grothendieck’s extensive work both on higher stacks but also the Grothendieck-Teichmuller theory. Here the theory is awaiting clear indications what higher Galois theory might mean.
